From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Wed, 16 Mar 1994 08:04:02 -0600 (CST)
Subject: [#94-3-208] LANG: Evolution of Language

Simon! writes:

> When people use the term
> "linguistics" (in academia, at least), it is the study of grammar and sound
> that they are most often referring to.  For these sorts of systems, it is
> much harder to find support for evolution: what, for example, would be the
> "niche" being filled by a language changing from SOV to SVO word order,
> as German is doing?

I would think that's pretty straightforward.  Sentences expressed in SVO
order are apparently "fitter" (more easily understood, more likely to be
transmitted, etc) than those expressed in SOV order.  If we treat the
"population" as the sentence structures used at a particular time, and
"reproduction" as the choice in an individual of a structure to use
to express a thought (surely based on a population of current and past
structures), we get reproduction biased toward fitter structures, which
is the driving force behind evolution.

derek

