Simon! Levy replies:
> [...] what, for example, would be the
>"niche" being filled by a language changing from SOV to SVO word order,
>as German is doing?

Some nations could have inherited brain structures that prefer SOV, with
others inclined towards SVO.  If the brain structure of a former SOV
nation mutated to SVO, traditional speech could lag behind (although for
most of a nation to mutate would take some time).  Or there could be a
number of SVO immigrants, who learnt the language as SOV, then became very
prominent in the population.  It would be as if a SVO niche was waiting to
be filled.

Still, it's not really a niche existing out there independently of the
genes.  It would really just be a part of human evolution, with the genes
the ultimate replicators rather than memes.  It's not like the memetic
evolution of scientific theories, religious concepts, jokes, widget
designs, words, etc, where memes replicate independently of genes.

>Of course, just because modern linguists don't focus on word meanings all
>that much doesn't mean that word meanings (and their evolution) aren't
>an interesting part of *language*.  So, to repeat myself, I think it makes
>sense to say that languages evolve to fill a niche, as long as you keep
>the focus on word meaning, and not formal structure.

This is how I was thinking -- linguists are less concerned with word
meanings than I'd assumed.  As for changes in formal structure, I'll trust
the linguists!

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

