Ray:
>> To claim that reductionism is wrong is philosophical 
>> nonsense. It overlooks the enormous success of reductionism 
>> in almost every field of study.

Steve Whitrow:
> One problem with reductionism is the "irreversibility paradox".
> According to reductionism, everything can be reduced to the 
> behaviour of particles.
> At the macrocosmic level there is clearly a thermodynamic arrow of 
> time, but at the microcosmic level time is reversible, with no 
> evidence of such an arrow.  Thus, the thermodynamic arrow does not 
> reduce to the microcosmic level.

Ray is correct.  The large areas of science are reductionist - you take
things apart to see how they work, until you arrive at some basal
description.  An attitude that is completely compatible with other
higher order descriptive/explanative summaries: spontaneous orders,
entropy, complexity, thermodynamics etc.  The top-down and bottom-up
descriptions/explanations are fully compatible with each other.

The idea that thermodynamics is incompatible with the reversible laws
of (reductionist) physics is an idea that everybody coming to terms with
thermodynamics seems to pass through (I did, anyway), but which is
completely false, nevertheless.  To predict, in principle, the behaviour
of anything you need two things.  First, knowledge of the rules of the
game (laws of physics).  Second, the state of the game at some moment
(initial conditions).  The thermodynamic arrow of time arises from the
ordered state of the game at start, _not_ from the rules of the game. 
The increase in entropy is due entirely to the Universe _starting out_
in a low entropy state.  Nothing to do with the reversibility of the
microscale or of reductionism.

