Date: Thu, 17 Mar 94 00:28:44 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-218] BIO: Wonderful Life 

That was interesting data on mitochondria genetics that Jay posted. 
Despite the differences it sounds like nuclear DNA and mitochondrial DNA
share a common origin sometime after the 20-amino acid transcription
evolved.

Thinking about the diversity of life while book-browsing about genetics
I came across _Wonderful Life_ by biologist Stephen Gould, which I've
been meaning to read for a while.  Named after the film _It's a
Wonderful Life_ starring James Stewart who, contemplating suicide, is
shown by an angel how the lives of other people would be different and
poorer if he'd never existed.  (Sounds like Dickens' _A Christmas
Carol_.)

Gould's book is about the Burgess Shale fossils.  The Burgess Shale
fossils date from around the time of the Cambrian Explosion some 570
million years ago with the appearance of the first hard bodied
organisms.  Many of the species represented don't fit into known classes
of phyla (mostly Arthropods, as far as I gather - I'm only 100 pages
into it), with bizarre (by modern zoological standards) anatomies and
body plans looking like the products of Dr Frankenstein.  The idea is
that in this first flowering of diversity hard bodied animals radiated
to fill empty niches.  Nature, in a manner of speaking, was
experimenting with different ideas.  Then Something Happened and most
of the forms were wiped out (yet another Mass Extinction?), leaving
justa few representatives, forerunners of most of the major classes.

Gould's thesis is that the success and failure in evolutionary stakes
is more chancy than generally appreciated.  The survivors of cataclysmic
extinctions are determined by chance rather than preadaptive fitness,
as the environment changes quickly.  If the tape of life were played
again, as he puts it, the history of evolution would be completely
different, each evolutionary path is of such low probability that it 
would never repeat itself.  Trivial events have profound consequences
and there is no inevitability about the path that evolution takes.  Had
different animals survived then, from the Burgess Shale evidence, we
might all have, say, 7 eyes and 13 legs.

Much as I want to believe Gould - and I do at the moment - haven't some
of the Burgess Shale anomalies, in the past few years, been reclassified
into conventional classes?  Does this significantly weaken his
proposition, or are they insignificant revisions?

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

