Subject: [#94-3-219] SCI: Inflation (was: PCR: Causality (Creation ...

Steve Whitrow:
> Mike Price forwards the interesting *New Scientist* article:

I'm glad you found it interesting and, I hope, informative.  I never did
get around to answering your earlier questions about inflation.

>> According to a popular but very speculative theory, first proposed
>> by the American physicist Alan Guth, when the Universe was about
>> 3 x 10^-44 seconds it began to expand exponentially fast.  This
>> so-called _inflationary epoch_ ended at about 3 x 10^-35 seconds.
>> [...] During inflation, space blew up, or "inflated", until it was
>> [at least] 10^50 time bigger than it had been.
> 
> One thing about inflation that I haven't seen described or explained,
> is just what it is that space inflates relative to.  (And "to what it
> had been" is no answer!)

You probably haven't seen it described or explained because all of
science is implicitly operational, as you said.  So when a scientist
says space has inflated they mean it has expanded by all measurable
criteria.  For instance it takes light only approx 10 nanoseconds to
cross a 3 meter gap.  Suppose (in some imaginary laboratory) we inflated
a 3 meter cube of space by the 10^50 factor mentioned above and fired
a laser beam through it.  Now the trip from one side to another takes
approx 3.10^42 seconds or 30 billion trillion trillion years.  (In
addition to assuming the imaginary experimenter is long-lived and
patient I am also assuming that the laser beam is sighted extremely
accurately, doesn't dissipate, attenuate or whatever.)  Light records
the increased dimensions of the old "3 meter" region of the lab, as do
other measuring devices also.  Feeding a perfectly aligned ruler through
from one side would (eventually!) reveal internal dimensions of approx
3.10^34 lyr.

> If all of space was equally expanding, any measuring rods, observers'
> heads, etc, would be expanding in exactly the same proportion; hence
> would not notice anything.

A measuring rod in a rapidly inflating/expanding regions would notice
something.  At the rates suggested by Guth and others the rod would be
ripped apart by geometric stresses.  In the lab example each subatomic
particle from a measuring rod originally spanning the gap ends up
separated from its nearest erstwhile neighbours by millions of trillion
of lyrs.  We record the remnants of inflation in the Universe today -
as the Hubble recession - precisely because the size of bound systems
like atomic electron orbits and galactic radii etc, in common with the
speed of light and other constants, do not inflate with the Universe.

