> fact difficult or impossible to successfully criticize. ...
> Another valid criticism of an idea, ceteris paribus, is its
> usefulness and applicability. While a little slipperier, we do
> actually use this criticism all the time, and the alternatives to
> the law of noncontradiction always flunk this one. 

  I disagree with this.  Picture someone being brainwashed by a cult
or political organization.  (I'm thinking specifically of the
scenarios described by Jack Chalker in his "Spirits of Flux and
Anchor" series.)  It may be that in a situation where a person is
severely punished for showing signs of holding or not holding certain
ideas, the most "useful" ideas are precisely the ones the people in
power want the subject to have--and it doesn't really matter if the
ideas contradict each other or not.

  A different but related problem that I see with PCR is that from
what little I know so far, the "starting set" of criticisms is
unspecified.  I think it has to be that way, because people who don't
practice PCR definitely have different sets of criticisms, and unless
they are told (dogmatically) "these are the criticisms you must start
with" when they are taught PCR, they will start with the ones they
already had.  (Let's call the criticisms they were using before they
learned about PCR "unstated criticisms.")  (I'm assuming here that
everyone agrees that people use this criticism method whether or not
they know it's called PCR.  Lots of people on this list have implied
this.  Talking with Max More it sounded like he disagreed with this,
but I think I probably misunderstood him.)
  By observation, I think some people do not use the law of
noncontradiction, or at least not all the time--not if it would cause
them to give up a cherished belief.  So then, the law of
noncontradiction is not in their unstated criticisms.  (Think of the
redneck stereotype.  "Midget commies.  Let's skin 'em."  Cult members
also seem to rank noncontradiction rather low.)  If these people were
taught PCR, and accepted it, and started practicing it, they would
still be starting from a set of criticisms that did not include the
law of noncontradiction.  
  It seems likely that someone starting from a set of criticisms that
did not include noncontradiction, but did include "any claim that Jim
Jones (or 'America') is wrong, must be false", probably would never
find noncontradiction to be very useful.  Their whole mind set would
be against it.  And unless they were stuck in a college Social Studies
class, or a deprogrammer's basement, they probably would not run into
very many situations where not using noncontradiction hurt them in
their daily lives.
  When I asked Max about this, he said several times that such people
would be "closed minded" and therefore not using PCR properly.  (I may
have misunderstood again.)  But forgetting about the "closed minded"
label for a second, in what way would these people not have used PCR
correctly?  They probably criticized noncontradiction and discarded it
as soon as they heard of it, just as I would criticize and discard an
assertion that I should give control of my life to the Supreme Bhagwan
Bill.
  So, in summary, is there anything about PCR that prevents people
from starting with criticisms which strengthen themselves and weaken
the case for more desirable (from our point of view) criticisms, to
the point that two people may, while using PCR, end up with
incompatible sets of criticisms?

