>Subject: [#94-3-196] PCR
>
>On Mon, 14 Mar 1994, Tim Starr wrote:
>
>> They didn't address realism, because it had fallen out of fashion.
>
>I beg your pardon? My understanding of realism is that it is a
>metaphysical theory, not an epistemological one.

There are positions known as "realist" in both metaphysics and episte-
mology.  In epistemology, realism is the view that knowing subjects
are directly aware of the objects of knowledge, which have independent
existence and identity apart from the knower.  This is in distinction
from both representationalism, a la Descartes, Locke, Hume, and most
Modern philosophers, which is the view that knowing subjects are
directly aware of representations of the objects of knowledge, and
from idealism, a la Kant, Hegel, etc., which is the view that the
objects of knowledge are constituted by knowing subjects; if not
actually brought into existence by them, then given identity by their
faculties.

>...I've read every word of nonfiction that Rand has written about
>epistemology, and in my reading Rand deals with Hume's problem
>alternatively by ignoring it or by misunderstanding it. Mostly by ignoring
>it, though. 

Correct.  She never gave a written presentation of her theory of
perception, so Kelley's is the first.

>No such thing as certainty beyond the shadow of a doubt exists.

This is self-refuting.

>... Certainty...is the attitude we have toward propositions
>that causes us to act as if those propositions were true. This is what
>we mean when we say "I am certain that x". We mean "I intend to act as
>if x were true".

Accepted, provisionally.

>Now, clearly, we can have different degrees of confidence in the truth
>of a matter...

I'm not sure what this can mean - that we intend to act as if some
things were really really true, and others not very true at all?
It seems more accurate to say that it will take more criticism to
get us to reject some positions than others which are less strongly
held.

>What does all this have to do with PanCritical Rationalism? PCR says
>that we do not ultimately base our knowledge on justifying ideas,
>because ultimately we have to come to some fundamental axiomatic
>idea(s) which we can only defend by appeals to faith or intuition.

Isn't the notion that "ultimately we have to come to some fundamental
axiomatic idea(s) which we can only defend by appeals to faith or
intuition" itself a fundamental axiomatic idea which can only be
defended by faith or intuition?  If not, isn't it founded upon such
a thing?

>Now it may seem that at least the law of noncontradiction may have
>special status in this scheme. But PCR insists that we hold the law of
>noncontradiction itself up to critical comparisons. We are safe in
>doing this because the law of noncontradiction always wins; it is in
>fact difficult or impossible to successfully criticize. (Side note: I
>haven't read anything about fuzzy logic yet.) The reason for this is
>that there are more criticisms than just the criticism of logical
>consistency (which is itself based on the law of noncontradiction,
>among others).

This doesn't follow.  It always wins because it's numerically less
than the total number of criticisms?  I'd say it always wins because
it must be presupposed in order to make any meaningful criticism.

>...the foundation of PCR is...sturdier than the foundation of at least Ayn
>Rand's version of objectivism. I am not familiar with Kelley's.  Perhaps
>if Tim could succinctly state the points of divergence between the two
>(that is, where does Kelley explicitly *disagree* with Rand?) I might be
>able to address this.

I don't think he does disagree with Rand.  What he does is articulate a
realist theory of perception to serve as the foundation for conceptual
knowledge.  I'll be happy to play tour guide to this theory, but I'll
need to know what direction you want to go in.

